export const resendEmailVerification = <ThrowOnError extends boolean = false>(options: Options<ResendEmailVerificationData, ThrowOnError>): RequestResult<ResendEmailVerificationResponses, ResendEmailVerificationErrors, ThrowOnError> => (options.client ?? client).post<ResendEmailVerificationResponses, ResendEmailVerificationErrors, ThrowOnError>({
    security: [{ scheme: 'bearer', type: 'http' }],
    url: '/v1/account/email/resend',
    ...options,
    headers: {
        'Content-Type': 'application/json',
        ...options.headers
    }
});

/**
 * Best time to post
 *
 * From this brand's own measured posts, per channel, on `timezone`: a day × hour grid (`slots`, one row per channel and slot with the post count, the average engagement and the lift against that channel's usual), the best weekday and 3-hour band where one beat the usual by 15% over at least 5 posts, and Instagram's followers-online hours when Instagram is connected. Raw numbers are never pooled across channels; below 15 measured posts nothing is named and `why` says how many there are. `day_of_week` 0 = Monday.
 */
export const getBestTime = <ThrowOnError extends boolean = false>(options?: Options<GetBestTimeData, ThrowOnError>): RequestResult<GetBestTimeResponses, GetBestTimeErrors, ThrowOnError> => (options?.client ?? client).get<GetBestTimeResponses, GetBestTimeErrors, ThrowOnError>({
    security: [{ scheme: 'bearer', type: 'http' }],
    url: '/v1/analytics/best-time',
    ...options
});

/**
 * Posting frequency vs engagement
 *
 * Per channel, completed weeks only (the last 180 days): for each posts-per-week, the weeks observed, the average engagement per post, the average engagement rate and the average follower change in those weeks. `frequency` has one row per (platform, posts_per_week); each channel names `best`, the highest cadence before engagement per post drops by 15%, once two cadences have 3+ measured weeks each.
 */
export const getPostingFrequency = <ThrowOnError extends boolean = false>(options?: Options<GetPostingFrequencyData, ThrowOnError>): RequestResult<GetPostingFrequencyResponses, GetPostingFrequencyErrors, ThrowOnError> => (options?.client ?? client).get<GetPostingFrequencyResponses, GetPostingFrequencyErrors, ThrowOnError>({
    security: [{ scheme: 'bearer', type: 'http' }],
    url: '/v1/analytics/posting-frequency',
    ...options
});

/**
 * Content decay
 *
 * How fast posts stop earning: for each age bucket (0-6h … 30d+), the average share of a post's final engagement (and, separately, of its final reach) reached by then, from posts with two or more readings and a settled reading 6+ days after posting. Per channel, plus `buckets` pooled across channels (a share is unitless, so pooling it is legitimate where pooling raw numbers is not).
 */
export const getContentDecay = <ThrowOnError extends boolean = false>(options?: Options<GetContentDecayData, ThrowOnError>): RequestResult<GetContentDecayResponses, GetContentDecayErrors, ThrowOnError> => (options?.client ?? client).get<GetContentDecayResponses, GetContentDecayErrors, ThrowOnError>({
    security: [{ scheme: 'bearer', type: 'http' }],
    url: '/v1/analytics/content-decay',
    ...options
});

/**
 * Follower history
 *
 * Follower count over time for every connected account (read once a day): one point per day, week or month with the change since the period before, up to a year (a longer range is cut and `warning` says so). A day whose read failed is a gap with its reason in `gaps`, never a 0. YouTube keeps its counts without a change (its API policy forbids derived numbers).
 */
export const getFollowerHistory = <ThrowOnError extends boolean = false>(options?: Options<GetFollowerHistoryData, ThrowOnError>): RequestResult<GetFollowerHistoryResponses, GetFollowerHistoryErrors, ThrowOnError> => (options?.client ?? client).get<GetFollowerHistoryResponses, GetFollowerHistoryErrors, ThrowOnError>({
    security: [{ scheme: 'bearer', type: 'http' }],
    url: '/v1/analytics/follower-history',
    ...options
});

/**
 * Inbox stats
 *
 * Across every connected messaging channel (WhatsApp, Messenger, Instagram, Telegram, SMS, LINE, Slack, Discord, email; X only with `include_x=true`, which X bills per message read): messages received and sent per day (`timeseries`) and per channel (`byPlatform`), a day × hour `heatmap` on `timezone` with the busiest hours (`dow` 1 = Monday), `responseTime` (median, p90, p99, mean, fastest, slowest and a histogram, measured from the other person's message to the brand's next reply in the same conversation), reply rate, the receiving accounts by load (`topAccounts`), per-conversation rows (`conversations`) and who is `waiting` now. Hermoso keeps each message's time and direction, never its text, from when the inbox was first read; `coverage` says since when and which channels could not be read.
 */
export const getInboxStats = <ThrowOnError extends boolean = false>(options?: Options<GetInboxStatsData, ThrowOnError>): RequestResult<GetInboxStatsResponses, GetInboxStatsErrors, ThrowOnError> => (options?.client ?? client).get<GetInboxStatsResponses, GetInboxStatsErrors, ThrowOnError>({
    security: [{ scheme: 'bearer', type: 'http' }],
    url: '/v1/analytics/inbox',
    ...options
});
